{"causes": ["His father, Hydarnes, held a powerful position as a satrap and was instrumental in Darius I's ascension, providing Hydarnes II with a privileged military background.", "The Achaemenid Empire's ongoing expansionist ambitions into the Aegean region necessitated a strong military command structure.", "The outbreak of the Second Persian Invasion of Greece under Xerxes I created the primary context for Hydarnes II to demonstrate his military capabilities."], "effects": ["Hydarnes II's successful flanking maneuver at Thermopylae directly led to the Persian victory over the Spartan-led Greek forces in 480 BCE.", "His tactical achievement opened the route for the Persian army to advance into central Greece and subsequently burn Athens.", "The Battle of Thermopylae, heavily influenced by Hydarnes II's actions, became a lasting symbol of heroic resistance and strategic vulnerability in military history."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "hydarnes-i", "sourceName": "Hydarnes (father)", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "hydarnes-ii", "targetName": "Hydarnes II", "context": "Hydarnes I, a prominent satrap and conspirator, provided the familial and political foundation for Hydarnes II's military career within the Achaemenid Empire."}, {"sourceSlug": "xerxes-i", "sourceName": "Xerxes I", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "hydarnes-ii", "targetName": "Hydarnes II", "context": "King Xerxes I entrusted Hydarnes II with a critical command during the Second Persian Invasion of Greece, particularly at the Battle of Thermopylae in 480 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "hydarnes-ii", "sourceName": "Hydarnes II", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "battle-of-thermopylae", "targetName": "Battle of Thermopylae", "context": "Hydarnes II commanded the Immortals in the decisive flanking maneuver that led to the Persian victory at the Battle of Thermopylae in 480 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "achaemenid-empire", "sourceName": "Achaemenid Empire", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "hydarnes-ii", "targetName": "Hydarnes II", "context": "Hydarnes II served as a high-ranking military commander within the Achaemenid Empire during its zenith in the 5th century BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "hydarnes-ii", "sourceName": "Hydarnes II", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "thermopylae-greece", "targetName": "Thermopylae, Greece", "context": "Hydarnes II executed his most significant military action by leading the flanking maneuver at the Battle of Thermopylae in 480 BCE."}], "places": [{"name": "Thermopylae, Greece", "role": "Major battle site"}, {"name": "Persia", "role": "Homeland and origin"}, {"name": "Achaemenid Empire", "role": "Sphere of influence"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Hydarnes II", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "hydarnes-ii"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q19568922", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydarnes_the_Younger"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Hydarnes II's strategic command at Thermopylae directly enabled the Persian victory, opening central Greece to invasion and dramatically altering the immediate trajectory of the Greco-Persian Wars. His actions exemplify the critical impact of military leadership and tactical innovation on historical outcomes.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-20T04:13:26Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44672738302", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Hydarnes II, also known as Hydarnes the Younger was a Persian commander of the Achaemenid Empire in the 5th century BC. He was the son of Hydarnes, satrap of the Persian empire and one of the seven conspirators against Gaumata.", "newValue": "Hydarnes II, also known as Hydarnes the Younger, distinguished himself as a pivotal Persian commander within the Achaemenid Empire during the 5th century BCE, a period defined by the epochal Greco-Persian Wars. As the son of Hydarnes, a prominent satrap and one of the seven conspirators who elevated Darius I to the throne, Hydarnes II inherited a legacy of military and political influence. His significance is indelibly linked to his decisive role in the Persian victory at the Battle of Thermopylae in 480 BCE, where his strategic acumen directly contributed to the outflanking and annihilation of the Spartan-led Greek forces.

His most celebrated action occurred during the second Persian invasion of Greece under Xerxes I. At Thermopylae, while the main Persian army faced fierce resistance from the Greek hoplites in the narrow pass, Hydarnes II was tasked with leading the elite Immortals along the Anopaia path, a treacherous mountain trail. Guided by a local Malian named Ephialtes, Hydarnes successfully navigated his contingent to the rear of the Greek positions, a maneuver that rendered the Greek defensive strategy untenable and sealed the fate of Leonidas's Spartans.

The strategic success orchestrated by Hydarnes II at Thermopylae had profound immediate consequences, opening the path for the Persian advance into central Greece and the subsequent sacking of Athens. While ultimately the Persian invasion was repelled at Salamis and Plataea, Hydarnes's tactical brilliance at Thermopylae showcased the sophisticated military capabilities of the Achaemenid Empire and forced the Greeks to adapt their defensive strategies. His actions underscored the critical importance of intelligence and flank maneuvers in ancient warfare, a lesson that resonated through subsequent military engagements."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-20T04:13:26Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44672738302", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "550 BCE"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "550 BCE"}], "newValue": ["His father, Hydarnes, held a powerful position as a satrap and was instrumental in Darius I's ascension, providing Hydarnes II with a privileged military background.", "The Achaemenid Empire's ongoing expansionist ambitions into the Aegean region necessitated a strong military command structure.", "The outbreak of the Second Persian Invasion of Greece under Xerxes I created the primary context for Hydarnes II to demonstrate his military capabilities."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-20T04:13:26Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44672738302", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "Classical"}], "newValue": ["Hydarnes II's successful flanking maneuver at Thermopylae directly led to the Persian victory over the Spartan-l